{"name":"Energy Peak","hex":"#bb5f60","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"energy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The impetus behind all motion and all activity.","The capacity to do work."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/energy"},{"term":"peak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A point; the sharp end or top of anything that terminates in a point; as, the peak, or front, of a cap.","The highest value reached by some quantity in a time period."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To raise the point of (a gaff) closer to perpendicular.","To raise an oar out of the water, fitting the handle into a round slot or socket located at the bottom of the boat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Energy","blocks":[{"text":"Energy is the quantitative property that is transferred to a body or to a physical system, recognizable in the capacity to do work and in the form of heat and light. Energy is a conserved quantity – the law of conservation of energy states that the total energy of an isolated system remains constant, although it can be converted in form. The unit of measurement for energy in the International System of Units (SI) is the joule (J)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Plasma_globe_60th.jpg/330px-Plasma_globe_60th.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_zoom.jpg/500px-Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_zoom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_zoom.jpg/1280px-Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_zoom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common